I'm thinking that there may be a disconnect between the job the education system is doing and the actual job that students are trying to get done. That seems to me to be a real possibility. So if we look at the job that students are trying to get done, it's probably something along the lines of: “I want to learn things that matter.” That's my take. And that's the job that students are trying to get done. And then perhaps another one is: “I want to earn money” — because most students don't get paid while studying. They often have to work part-time jobs to survive. So earning money is a key goal. And then there's also socialization. Students want to hang out with friends. They want to fit in. They want to feel like they belong. So these are three core jobs that students are trying to get done.

Now, going back to the original question, the user asked whether AI tools can replace teachers. So I'm wondering if we've already answered that question implicitly. Because if the job students are trying to get done is to learn things that matter and earn money and feel socially connected, then AI tools alone cannot fulfill these jobs. Teachers play a crucial role in helping students achieve these goals. So even though AI can assist with learning, it cannot fully replace teachers. Especially since teaching isn't just about delivering information. Teaching involves understanding student emotions, building relationships, creating inclusive classrooms, adapting to individual needs, and guiding students through challenges. These aspects are deeply human and hard to replicate with machines. So I think the answer to the question is no — AI tools cannot replace teachers, especially when considering the full scope of what students are trying to get done.

